    "A Bayesian posterior of 0.71 emerges after upward updates from consolidation signals and turnout expectations, tempered by persistent poll divergence and structural electoral biases."
  ],
  "confidence": "medium",
  "narrative": "Starting from a 44% historical baseline for a constitutional supermajority, consensus shifts toward the TISZA-led opposition are possible but not assured. The combination of favorable polls for the opposition, turnout dynamics, and potential new entrants yields a posterior probability around 0.71 with notable uncertainty remaining.",
  "justification": {
    "anchor": {
      "probability": 0.44,
      "basis": "Historical frequency of two-thirds majorities in the post-1990 Hungarian National Assembly; reference class defined as achieving 134+ seats in 199-seat body."
    },
    "factor_updates": [
      {
        "factor": "Opposition consolidation around the TISZA platform",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Polling signals a credible opposition lead, which translates into higher seat potential if voting blocs cohere and turnout supports opposition."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Poll heterogeneity and cross-pollster divergence",
        "direction": "neutral",
        "magnitude": "weak",
        "rationale": "Divergent polls inject uncertainty; they prevent strong confidence in any single outcome and attenuate the likelihood of a guaranteed 134 seats."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Electoral system effects and seat allocation bias against 2/3",
        "direction": "down",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Structural features tend to make a 2/3 outcome less likely than a simple or near-simple majority, unless momentum is overwhelming."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Potential influx of new entrants (e.g., Our Homeland Movement)",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Extra parties can shift seat shares unpredictably; if allied with the opposition or the ruling bloc, they could alter the path to 134."
      },
      {
        "factor": "Turnout dynamics favoring opposition blocs",
        "direction": "up",
        "magnitude": "moderate",
        "rationale": "Higher turnout among opposition-leaning voters could push seats toward the 134 threshold, especially in battleground districts."
      }
    ],
    "synthesis_rationale": "Starting from a 0.44 anchor, each factor was treated as an update with approximate magnitude based on the provided forecasts: three factors push up (consolidation, Our Homeland entry, turnout) and two moderate constraints dampen the odds (poll divergence, structural bias). The aggregated effect yields a posterior around 0.71; however, high heterogeneity across polls and seat-models yields a wide uncertainty band, hence the calibrated range. The forecast is not deterministic; small shifts in turnout or third-party dynamics could swing the outcome."
  }
}

